

⚡ What PermissionLayer Is

PermissionLayer enforces the organism’s safety rules, operational limits, and allowed behaviors based on the current regime.

It is the gatekeeper.

It prevents:

runaway warp fields

gravity spikes

stress overload

charge explosions

field collapse cascades

It ensures the organism stays inside its engineered capacity.

⚡ What PermissionLayer Actually Does

It has three major responsibilities, each one essential.

🜁 1. Enforce Permissions — “What is allowed right now?”

Every organism defines:

allowed warp factors

allowed stress levels

allowed charge flux

allowed gravity gradients

allowed duty cycles

The PermissionLayer checks:

current regime

current field values

organism’s permission rules

Then decides:

allow

clamp

reduce

shut down

This is the behavior enforcement step.

🜂 2. Check Safety Envelope — “Are we still within safe limits?”

The safety envelope is the organism’s hard boundary.

Examples:

max_warp_factor

max_stress_load

max_charge_flux

max_gravity_shear

If the organism exceeds any of these:

the PermissionLayer triggers a clamp

or forces a shutdown

or escalates to failure regime

This is the safety guardian step.

🜃 3. Clamp Fields — “Reduce dangerous values immediately.”

When the organism enters critical or exotic regimes, the PermissionLayer may:

reduce warp intensity

flatten gravity gradients

dissipate charge

redistribute stress

slow field evolution

This prevents catastrophic collapse.

This is the damage‑control step.

⚡ Why PermissionLayer Matters

Without it:

warp fields would exceed safe curvature

gravity gradients would spike uncontrollably

stress loads would tear the organism apart

charge flux could go runaway

the simulation would crash constantly

organisms would behave like unstable bombs

PermissionLayer is the immune system that prevents self‑destruction.

It is the law that keeps the organism inside its engineered design.

It is the guardian that protects the simulation from catastrophic failure.

⚡ How It Fits Into the Engine Flow

RegimeOrchestrator → determines mode
PermissionLayer    → enforces what is allowed in that mode
FieldDynamics      → evolves fields within those limits
CrashAnalyzer      → checks if limits were violated anyway

The PermissionLayer is the middle shield between normal operation and catastrophic collapse.



